The conformance set of the resolution of a module manifest (seam 3)
====================================================================

Each directory here is one case. The function test_manifest_conformance in
tests/run.sh runs each case through mcp_load (lib/mcp.sh) and compares the
result with the expected file. A later Go core must give the same results.

This file gives version 1 of the dump format.


The files of a case
-------------------

module.toml    The manifest.
context        Lines "<key>=<value>":
                 name=<the module name>
                 origin=shared | local
               "shared" puts the module into mcp/, "local" into mcp.local/.
settings.toml  Optional. The setting values, as config.toml holds them.
               The runner reads the keys of the [settings] of the manifest
               from this file. It runs in the directory of the case, so a
               relative path names a file of the case (the check file).
expected       The dump, when the status is 0.
expected.err   The status and the error, when the status is not 0.

The existence of a Podman secret is not an input: the start checks it,
not the resolution.


The dump (expected)
-------------------

- One line "<key>=<value>" for each item. The file is UTF-8, with a
  newline after each line.
- A value has these escapes, in the key too: "\" is "\\", a newline is
  "\n", and "=" is "\=". These three are enough: the reader refuses each
  other control character, and allows a newline only in a help text.
- An empty value gives no line. Exception: each setting of the manifest
  has its "settings.<KEY>" line, also with an empty value.
- The lines are sorted by bytes (LC_ALL=C sort), after the escapes.
- A list gives one line for each item, with the index from 0. The items
  keep the order of the manifest.

The keys:

description                  The description of the module.
settings.<KEY>               The value after the default and lowercase. An
                             empty value counts as not set, and gets the
                             default. A list setting is its items joined
                             with a comma.
settings.<KEY>.required      1 for a required setting.
settings.<KEY>.help          The help of the setting.
field.<KEY>.<field>          A field of the check url or url-base
                             (MCP_FIELD): scheme, host, port, hostport,
                             base. A list setting has no fields.
items.<KEY>                  The items of a list setting (MCP_ITEMS), each
                             once, joined with a space.
servers.<n>.name             The name of a server.
servers.<n>.url              Its URL.
servers.<n>.caller           1 when the server gets the caller token.
servers.<n>.description      Its description: the description of the
                             module when the server has none.
secrets.<n>                  The Podman name of a secret that must exist
                             before the start: "<module>_<local name>".
                             A secret with for_each gives one line for
                             each item. These lines come after the lines
                             of the other secrets.
secret_help.<Podman name>    The help of that secret.
secret.<local name>          The Podman name for each local name: the
                             secrets, the own secrets and mcp_caller
                             (MCP_SECRET).
own_secrets.<n>              The Podman name of a secret that the code of
                             the module makes.
caller_secret                The Podman name of the caller token,
                             "<module>_mcp_caller", when a server has
                             caller = true.
image.<local name>           The image reference.
egress.<n>                   A host that the agent can reach.


Lists and substitutions
-----------------------

The value of a setting goes through these steps, in this order:

1. The value from config.toml or the environment. An empty value counts
   as not set, and the default applies.
2. With lowercase = true, the value becomes lowercase.
3. A list setting (list = true) splits into items. The reader gives an
   array of config.toml as its items joined with a comma. The unit
   replaces each comma with a space, and splits at spaces and tabs.
   Empty items go away. When an item is there two times, the first one
   stays, in its place. So "Red, blue  red,,green" with lowercase gives
   the items red, blue, green.
4. The check, for each item of a list. The first item that fails gives
   the error of the setting.
5. The fields of the check (url and url-base only, not for a list).

The value of a list setting is its items joined with a comma.

A template is text with these substitutions:

- {SETTING}: the value of the setting. The setting must have a check.
- {SETTING.field}: a field of its check (see "The built-in checks").
- {item}: the item, in a table with for_each only.
- {{ and }}: a literal { and }.

Any other { or } is an error. The unit does not expand a substituted
value again. An empty field of a setting with a value (port, for a URL
with no port) gives empty text. A setting with no value has these
results:

- in the name or the URL of a server: missing-setting (status 1);
- in an egress host: the unit leaves out that host;
- in a description or a help: empty text.

At status 1, the help of a secret with for_each can use a setting that
failed its check. That setting gives empty text there, as a setting with
no value does.


The error (expected.err)
------------------------

The first line is "status=<n>", the status of mcp_load:

1   A setting is missing or fails a check. The module stays out of the
    start, and agentbox removes its access.
2   The module is broken.

Then one line for each problem:

- Status 1: "<KEY>: <code>", one line for each setting that is missing
  or fails its check, in the order of the manifest.
- Status 2: "module.toml:<line>: <code>" for the first error only. The
  line is the line of the key, or of its table header. A key with no
  line gives "module.toml: <code>": a key in an inline table, a table
  with no keys that is not the first table of its kind, and the error
  reserved-name.

The first error: the errors of the reader come first, in the order of
the reader. Then the manifest unit checks in this order, and stops at the
first error:

1. bad-format, reserved-name.
2. Each setting in its order: unknown-check.
3. Each secret in its order: reserved-secret, and bad-substitution for
   {item} in a secret with no for_each.
4. no-server.
5. The templates, before the values: each secret with for_each
   (bad-secret-name for a name with no {item}), each server, then egress
   (bad-substitution, unchecked-setting). The declared part checks them
   too.
6. The settings, in their order: a missing setting or a failed check.
7. Each secret with for_each whose list setting passed step 6, in its
   order: bad-value, bad-secret-name.
8. If step 6 found a problem, the unit stops with status 1.
9. Each server in its order: the substitutions of the name and the URL
   (bad-value). A setting with no value in the name or the URL is a
   missing setting, and the unit goes to the next server. Then
   bad-server-name, duplicate-server, the substitutions of the
   description, pipe-in-server.
10. If step 9 found a missing setting, the unit stops with status 1. The
    "<KEY>: missing-setting" lines of step 9 come after the lines of
    step 6, in the order of the servers.
11. The substitutions of egress (bad-value), then bad-egress-host.


The error codes
---------------

Status 1:

missing-setting     A required setting has no value and no default, or
                    the name or the URL of a server uses a setting with
                    no value.
url, url-base,      The value fails the built-in check of this name
port, name, file    (the check of the setting). For a list setting,
                    one item fails. A default can fail too.

The built-in checks:

url        http:// or https://, a host, an optional port, and an optional
           path of the characters A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ ~ : / % + = , ! * -.
           No user@, no query, no fragment. The host is one or more
           labels with a "." between them. A label is A-Z a-z 0-9 and
           "-", with no "-" at the start or the end. A port is 1 to 65535,
           with no 0 at the start. The fields: scheme, host, port (empty
           with no port), hostport (the host, and ":port" when there is
           a port), base ("scheme://hostport" and the path, with no "/" at
           the end).
url-base   The same as url, with no path. The value can have one "/" at the end.
           base is "scheme://hostport".
port       A port, as above.
name       A-Z a-z 0-9 and "-", with no "-" at the start or the end, no
           "--", and at most 39 characters. The value does not change.
file       A file that exists and that agentbox can read.

Status 2, from the manifest unit (lib/mcp.sh):

bad-format          "format" is 0 or lower. The first format is 1.
reserved-name       The module has the name "git". The prefix "git_" of
                    the secret names is for the modules from git. The
                    name is not a key, so the error has no line.
reserved-secret     The manifest has [secrets.mcp_caller]. agentbox makes
                    this secret for a server with caller = true. The line
                    of the table header.
no-server           The manifest has no [[servers]]. The line of
                    "format".
bad-server-name     A server name is not lowercase letters, digits and
                    "-", with a letter or a digit first. A "|" in a
                    server name gives this code.
duplicate-server    Two servers of the manifest have the same name. The
                    line of the second [[servers]].
unknown-check       The check of a setting is not a built-in check.
                    The line of its [settings.<KEY>] header.
bad-substitution    A substitution is not {SETTING}, {SETTING.field} or
                    {item}: a setting that the manifest does not have, a
                    field that its check does not give (a list setting
                    has no fields), {item} in a table with no for_each,
                    or a { or } with no pair (write {{ and }}). Also a
                    for_each that names a setting with no list = true.
unchecked-setting   A substitution or a for_each uses a setting with no
                    check.
bad-value           A substituted value has a "|", a control character
                    or a space.
bad-secret-name     The name of a secret with for_each has no {item}.
                    Or a name from for_each has a character other than
                    A-Z a-z 0-9 _ . -, or another secret of the module
                    (also mcp_caller) has this name. The line of the
                    table header.
bad-egress-host     An egress host after the substitutions is not a host
                    name, .domain or host:port (the rule of the egress
                    hosts of agentbox). The line of "egress".
pipe-in-server      The URL or the description of a server has a "|".
                    The description can come from the description of the
                    module, for a server with no description; then the
                    line is the line of "description".

Status 2, from the TOML reader (lib/toml-reader.py; the reader set in
tests/toml/manifest/ has the cases):

syntax, newer-format, multi-line-string, unknown-key, dot-in-key,
quoted-key, bad-name, type, table, missing-key, float, date,
control-character
